THE SAME COMPARISON ON THE SERVER'S OWN PER-ROUND CLOCK, which resolves about
four times finer than the 0.25 s GAME_TIME sampling above:
  control: 11 comparable rounds, 14.6871s..14.7849s, spread 0.0978s  <- the NOISE FLOOR, measured
  steer  : 9 comparable rounds, longest 24.7693s
  the steering arm beat the control's longest round by +9.9844s (required: more than 0.5000s = 5x the control's own spread)

VERDICT: A STEERING KEYPRESS REACHED THE SERVER, ON TWO INDEPENDENT COUNTS.
  1. The server kept the cycle alive for longer than a cycle that never turns
     can survive in this arena -- a bound the control arm measured with no key
     pressed at all. The server simulates that cycle, so the extra life is the
     server's cycle having turned.
  2. And some rounds ended SOONER than the control's fastest. Nothing else is
     in the arena: a cycle going straight cannot reach a wall before the rim,
     so an early death is a direction change. This is what rules out the key
     having been bound to the brake, which could only ever delay the rim.

  ONE BINDING HAS TO EXPLAIN BOTH TAILS, and that is the force of it. A brake
  explains the long survivals only. Anything that merely killed the cycle
  early explains the short ones only. Only a change of DIRECTION explains
  15.49s and 2.62s coming out of the same key. ONE early
  round is sufficient for that, which is why this file requires one of each
  and not a majority of either -- the counts above are not a statistic.
